A||||4||Voytek||Stubborn O2 Sensor? No problem...||||7||
Z||000000||Voytek||05-18-2001||11:34 AM||||Some tips if anyone ever has problems replacing the O2 sensor:<BR>So I got the code 44 the other day.  Either a vacuum leak or a bad oxygen sensor.  Hmmm...  Well, the car didn't run at high idle all the time SOOOOO, must be the sensor.  Sprayed WD40 once.   Sprayed WD40 twice......  Still nothing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> By now I was using a big wrench with an extension pipe and very afraid of ripping out the whole socket!  Ok, the Y-pipe must come out.  About an hour later the pipe was out.  I put the darn thing in a vice and turned the pipe.  Nothing.  Now it felt like the vice would rip out of the table any second.  What to do...what to do...<BR>When in stress, a hammer is always a good reliever.  Good bye oxygen sensor!  Ok, so now the top was ripped out but the rest was still in.  Now it was time to combine the hammer with a long, slim pointy object.  This combination helped me bust out the rest of the ceramics from the inside of the sensor.  Now what?!  The nut/thread part is still in!!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  Well, a large drill bit and a drill (of course) took out most of the remaining sensor.  Only the thread was left now and still seized!!  A small file (to make a cut/groove in the thread), a sharp object and a hammer did the rest of the job (hammered the sharp object between the remaining O2 thread and the socket thread).  IT'S OUT!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (patting myself on the back)<BR>Best of all, the new sensor cost me $34 CDN and a shop wanted about $100 to replace it.<BR>So if anyone ever wonders how to get the old O2 sensor out, here it is!||||reg||7
Z||000001||Raydar||05-18-2001||12:02 PM||||Jeez.<BR>That sounds like <I>at least</I> $66 worth of pain-in-the-@ss.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Did you put anti-sieze compound on the new one? <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||05-18-2001||01:07 PM||||Your punctuation is in the wrong place - it should be:<P>Stubborn O2 sensor, no? Problem!<P>I hate to ask now that you are done, but did you run the car first to get the pipe hot before you tried the WD40?<P>Most of the time, if the thread grease was on there, and if you run the car a few minutes, the O2 will come off without a fuss.<P>If you do end up taking the pipe out, then heat is still your best friend. get a swirl port propane torch - they get almost hot enough to braze with on propane - heat the pipe around it up good, then blast it with wd40 or water to get a thermal shock happening, then heat it up good and take the wrench to it.<P>  Its very risky to use the torch on the O2 sensor while the pipe is still in the car, though, cause the gas lines and vapor canister are very nearby.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Voytek||05-18-2001||04:08 PM||||There was SOME kind of compound on the new sensor.  I didn't put any extra.<P>The pipe was warm when I started but, in order to avoid burning myself (and the access to the 02 IS a bi*ch) I did not get it hot.<BR>Oh, and after about 200,000 km (or about 125,000 miles) there was no original grease/thread compound left.  In fact when I finally ripped it out, it was just metal stuck to metal.  Thanks for the suggestions, though.  I'll look for this thread when I get a new sensor in about 14 years  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  (my car is an '87).||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||05-18-2001||04:13 PM||||20 to 30k miles - replace it often and early and be glad its only $25 to $30. For most cars they are &gt; $100.<P> the O2 sensor is THE primary input to the ECM - now that you've done it once treat it like a spark plug or air filter - regular maintance item. Next time it wont be bonded to the pipe and it will come right out.<P>  but you do need to warm it up till the pipe is hot.||||reg||1
